\title{Cartesian product of types}
\date{2026-02-26T18:45:00Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{In Disco, we can take the [Cartesian product](0078) of [types](002B) as well.  For example, if we take the Cartesian product of a set of natural numbers and a set of Booleans, the type of the resulting elements is \code{ℕ × Bool}:}

\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> {1,2,3} >< {T,F}
{(1, F), (1, T), (2, F), (2, T), (3, F), (3, T)}
Disco> :type {1,2,3} >< {T,F}
{1, 2, 3} >< {T, F} : Set(ℕ × Bool)<<<

\p{In this example, \code{ℕ × Bool} is the type of the elements, which look like pairs such as \code{(1, F)}.  In general, the type \code{A × B} (also written \code{A >< B} or \code{A * B}) has values that look like ordered pairs \code{(a,b)}.}
